--TEST--
A reply that carried no body reads as an empty `string` and empty `bytes` rather than as an absence, and one byte is the first body there is — `rule:core-api/shape-rules`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The claim is *a bound asserted on both sides*: the emptiest body and the
// smallest one that is not, named together. `Core\Http\Response` has no
// nullable body reader, so "the origin sent nothing" and "the origin sent an
// empty document" are one answer on purpose — a program that has to tell them
// apart reads the status.
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/gone", 204);
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/one", 200, {body: "x"});

var $gone = Core\Http\Client::delete("https://api.example.com/gone");
echo $gone->status(), " ", Core\Bytes::length($gone->bytes()), " ", Core\Str::length($gone->text()), "\n";

var $one = Core\Http\Client::get("https://api.example.com/one");
echo $one->status(), " ", Core\Bytes::length($one->bytes()), " ", Core\Str::length($one->text()), "\n";

// A `HEAD` never has a body to read, and that is the same answer rather than a
// second shape a caller has to handle.
var $head = Core\Http\Client::head("https://api.example.com/one");
echo Core\Bytes::length($head->bytes()), "\n";

// The absence a reply *does* spell is a header it never carried, which is what
// makes the body's emptiness a decision rather than an oversight.
echo $gone->header("content-type") ?? "absent", "\n";
--EXPECT--
204 0 0
200 1 1
1
absent
